@startuml HAPLib
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' HAPLib - structure only.
'
' Names, relations and the role of each class. No fields and no methods: the
' headers carry those and would only go stale here. What this diagram is for is
' the SHAPE of the library - which class knows about which, and how a frame off
' a radio becomes a reading somebody can read.
'
' It is drawn to the same conventions as HCoreLib/Docs/ClassDiagram.puml, and
' the two are meant to be read together: HAPLib sits ON HCoreLib and never the
' other way round.
'
' THE THREE ROLES
'   «boundary»  something on the EDGE of the process - a radio, a flash
'               partition, an entropy source. There are only those THREE
'               places, and that is the point: almost nothing in this library
'               touches hardware, which is what lets a whole simulated tree of
'               nodes run in a host test. (Five boxes carry the stereotype -
'               two of them are the transport's interchangeable backends.)
'   «control»   logic and coordination - the mechanisms with an opinion about
'               what happens next.
'   «entity»    a value, a record or a message payload that gets passed around.
'
' WHAT IS COLLAPSED, so nobody reads an absence as a claim. Three groups are
' drawn as one box each, and in every case the box is the unit a translation
' unit actually includes:
'
'   * the 27 message payloads (HAPPing, HAPBindAnnounce, HAPOtaRequest, ...)
'     appear as their five FAMILY headers. A reporter has no business compiling
'     the link messages, and thirty boxes would bury the shape this is for.
'     The three pieces they are BUILT from - HAPInstanceDescriptor,
'     HAPValueEntry, HAPPortRef - stay visible as HAPMessageParts, because
'     every family depends on that one and the arrows are the point.
'   * HAPWriter and HAPReader appear as HAPCodec, the header that declares both.
'   * HAPClassSpec and HAPPortSpec appear as HAPClasses - they ARE the table.
'     What a class MEANS lives in Docs/Classes/, not here: giving BatteryState
'     a second out port changes that table and nothing on this diagram.
'   * HLog is not drawn. Six .cpp files log; not one of them does anything
'     differently because of it, and six arrows into a diagnostic would claim a
'     shape the library does not have.
'
' Private nested structs (Slot, Peer, Page, Entry, Block) are not drawn at all.
' They are implementation detail of the class that owns them, and naming them
' here would promise a stability their headers do not.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam ArrowColor #33556B
skinparam package {
  BorderColor #8FA6B4
  BackgroundColor #FBFCFD
}

skinparam class<<boundary>> {
  BorderColor #1F6F8B
  BackgroundColor #E8F4F8
}
skinparam class<<control>> {
  BorderColor #99621E
  BackgroundColor #FBF0E0
}
skinparam class<<entity>> {
  BorderColor #3E7D4F
  BackgroundColor #E9F5EC
}
skinparam class<<external>> {
  BorderColor #7A7A8C
  BackgroundColor #F0F0F4
}

hide empty members
hide circle

package "Vocabulary" {
  class HAP <<entity>>
  class HAPMac <<entity>>
  class HAPPath <<entity>>
  class HAPCrc16 <<control>>
  class HAPRandom <<boundary>>

  note bottom of HAP
    Not a class: the header every other one
    includes. Constants, the message enum,
    the flag bits, the result codes - the
    words the protocol is written in.
  end note
}

package "Wire format" {
  class HAPCodec <<control>>
  class HAPFrame <<entity>>
  class HAPMessageParts <<entity>>
  class HAPBindMessages <<entity>>
  class HAPDataMessages <<entity>>
  class HAPControlMessages <<entity>>
  class HAPLinkMessages <<entity>>
  class HAPServiceMessages <<entity>>
  class HAPMessages <<entity>>

  note bottom of HAPMessages
    An umbrella header, not a class: it includes
    the five families for code that dispatches on
    HAPFrame::message() and therefore touches most
    of the catalogue.
  end note
}

package "This node" {
  class HAPClasses <<entity>>
  class HAPInstance <<entity>>
  class HAPNode <<entity>>
}

package "Mechanisms" {
  class HAPRouter <<control>>
  class HAPBinder <<control>>
  class HAPReporter <<control>>
  class HAPLinks <<control>>
  class HAPQueue <<control>>
  class HAPListenWindow <<control>>
  class HAPModel <<entity>>
  class HAPStore <<boundary>>
}

package "Transport" {
  class HAPITransport <<boundary>>
  class HAPEspNow <<boundary>>
  class HAPLoopback <<boundary>>
}

package "The whole protocol" {
  class HAPStack <<control>>
}

package "HCoreLib" <<Rectangle>> {
  class HValue <<external>>
  class HConfig <<external>>
  class HRtcStore <<external>>
  class HTimer <<external>>
  class HSystemUtils <<external>>
}

' --- Vocabulary ----------------------------------------------------------
HAPMac ..> HAP
HAPPath ..> HAP
HAPCodec ..> HAP
HAP ..> HValue : the five wire types ARE\nHValue's five, and HAPAssign\nis the one place a stored\nvalue changes TYPE

' --- Wire format ---------------------------------------------------------
' A payload knows its own bytes and nothing else. What surrounds it is
' HAPFrame's business, and when to send one is HAPStack's - which is why every
' arrow in this package points DOWN into the codec and never sideways.
HAPFrame ..> HAP
HAPFrame ..> HAPPath : carries two
HAPFrame ..> HAPCodec
HAPCodec ..> HValue : what a VALUE is\nread into and\nwritten from
HAPMessageParts ..> HAP
HAPMessageParts ..> HAPCodec
HAPMessageParts ..> HAPPath
HAPMessageParts *-- HValue : every VALUE on the wire\nIS an HValue
HAPBindMessages ..> HAPMessageParts
HAPDataMessages ..> HAPMessageParts
HAPControlMessages ..> HAPMessageParts
HAPLinkMessages ..> HAPMessageParts
HAPServiceMessages ..> HAPMessageParts
HAPMessages ..> HAPMessageParts
HAPMessages ..> HAPBindMessages
HAPMessages ..> HAPDataMessages
HAPMessages ..> HAPControlMessages
HAPMessages ..> HAPLinkMessages
HAPMessages ..> HAPServiceMessages

' --- This node -----------------------------------------------------------
' A descriptor names what a thing IS; HAPClasses says what that MEANS. Which is
' why ports are never transmitted and a node's descriptor stays small.
HAPClasses ..> HAP
HAPInstance ..> HAPClasses : ports come from the class
HAPInstance ..> HAPMessageParts
HAPInstance ..> HSystemUtils : every port remembers\nwhen it last moved
HAPNode *-- "0..8" HAPInstance
HAPNode ..> HAPMessages
HAPNode ..> HAPCrc16 : descriptorRev is a CRC\nover the descriptor

' --- Mechanisms ----------------------------------------------------------
HAPRouter ..> HAPFrame
HAPRouter ..> HAPMac
HAPBinder ..> HAPITransport : adds the encrypted peer
HAPBinder ..> HAPRouter
HAPBinder ..> HAPNode
HAPBinder ..> HAPFrame
HAPBinder ..> HAPBindMessages
HAPBinder ..> HAPRandom : the link key
HAPBinder ..> HTimer : four deadlines - the window,\nthe announce, the channel\ndwell, the confirmation
HAPReporter ..> HAPNode : reads the out ports
HAPReporter ..> HAPDataMessages
HAPReporter ..> HAPClasses
HAPReporter ..> HSystemUtils : when a report is due
HAPLinks ..> HAPNode
HAPLinks ..> HAPLinkMessages
HAPLinks ..> HAPClasses : a link is legal or it is not
HAPQueue ..> HAP
HAPQueue ..> HSystemUtils
HAPListenWindow ..> HAP
HAPListenWindow ..> HTimer
HAPModel ..> HAPMessages
HAPModel ..> HAPMac
HAPModel ..> HAPPath
HAPModel ..> HSystemUtils : how long since\na node was heard
HAPStore ..> HAPNode : names
HAPStore ..> HAPModel : who is out there
HAPStore ..> HAPRouter : the bind
HAPStore ..> HConfig : a file per thing kept
HAPStore ..> HRtcStore : the bind, mirrored\nso a wake need not\nmount a filesystem

' --- Transport -----------------------------------------------------------
' Virtual on purpose. It is the seam a host test substitutes a whole simulated
' network at, and the seam this project's own MeshRadio wraps to mount a
' filesystem before a frame that needs one is handled.
HAPITransport ..> HAP : a size and a key length,\nno message and no frame
HAPITransport ..> HAPMac
HAPITransport <|.. HAPEspNow
HAPITransport <|.. HAPLoopback

' --- The stack -----------------------------------------------------------
' Twenty lines connecting seven mechanisms, and they are the same twenty lines
' on every device in the ecosystem - which is what makes them library work
' rather than each application's.
HAPStack *-- HAPRouter
HAPStack *-- HAPBinder
HAPStack *-- HAPReporter
HAPStack *-- HAPLinks
HAPStack *-- HAPQueue
HAPStack *-- HAPListenWindow
HAPStack --> HAPITransport : not owned
HAPStack --> HAPNode : not owned
HAPStack --> HAPModel : optional -\na leaf must not\npay for one
HAPStack ..> HAPStore

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
note as Acyclic
  **This graph has no cycles, through headers or through .cpp files.**

  The library is a strict stack, and reading it bottom-up is reading the
  protocol itself:

  HAP -> HAPPath/HAPMac/HAPCodec -> HAPFrame/HAPMessageParts -> the five
  message families -> HAPInstance -> HAPNode -> the mechanisms -> HAPStack

  Nothing below ever names anything above it. Three places where that took a
  deliberate decision rather than luck:

  * **HAPRouter does not know what a node is.** It moves frames by path and
    nothing else, so HAPStore can persist a bind through it without the router
    ever learning there is a filesystem.
  * **HAPModel is a pointer on the stack, not a member.** A root wants one and
    a leaf must not pay close to a kilobyte per remote node for it - so the
    dependency is optional at runtime and absent from the header's weight.
  * **HAPITransport names no message and no frame.** It takes bytes and a
    MAC, and all it borrows from the vocabulary header is a size and a key
    length. The seam below the protocol cannot know what a frame is, or a test
    could not substitute a whole simulated network at it - and this project's
    MeshRadio could not wrap it to mount a filesystem before a frame that needs
    one is handled.

  **Both kinds are checked, and that distinction is the point.** A cycle
  through headers cannot compile, so it is found the day it is written. A cycle
  that closes through .cpp files compiles perfectly and survives for years - it
  is only ever found by looking.
end note

note as Diamond
  **No diamond, and nothing to solve.**

  Not one class in this library has two bases, and `virtual` inheritance
  appears nowhere. There is exactly ONE inheritance relation in the whole of
  HAPLib:

  * HAPITransport <- HAPEspNow | HAPLoopback

  and one more outside it, which is worth knowing because it is the reason the
  interface is virtual at all:

  * HAPITransport <- **MeshRadio** (HTHP/App) - a pass-through that mounts the
    filesystem before a frame that can change stored state reaches the protocol

  Everything else composes. HAPStack owns six mechanisms by value and holds
  three more by reference; not one of them is a base class. That is what keeps
  the object graph flat enough to reason about with no heap and no RTTI - the
  firmware that carries this library builds with RTTI off (ESP-IDF's default;
  HTHP's sdkconfig leaves `CONFIG_COMPILER_CXX_RTTI` unset), so `dynamic_cast`
  does not exist on target and a virtual base would have to be navigated by
  hand.
end note

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Both notes above are mechanically checkable, and were checked. Re-run either
' after changing an include, from THIS repository's root:
'
'   python Tests/HCoreLib/tools/checkdeps.py .         # add -v for every edge
'
' or from the directory holding both checkouts side by side:
'
'   python HCoreLib/tools/checkdeps.py HAPLib
'   python HCoreLib/tools/checkdeps.py HCoreLib
'
' and for the diamond half, which is one grep because the answer is "none":
'
'   grep -rnE "^ *class +[A-Za-z0-9_]+ *: *(public|protected|private)" \
'        --include=*.hpp . | grep -v Tests
'
' Every line it prints should name exactly one base.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

legend right
  |= role |= what it means |
  |<#E8F4F8> «boundary» | the edge of the process: a radio, a flash partition |
  |<#FBF0E0> «control» | logic and coordination, no state of its own worth naming |
  |<#E9F5EC> «entity» | a value, record or message payload that gets passed around |
  |<#F0F0F4> «external» | HCoreLib - this library sits on it, never the reverse |
endlegend

@enduml
